

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more thing I can't derive from the repo, then I'll bring you approaches.

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a [1mclassic script[0m ([38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m, no [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m), while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS
  [38;5;153mrequire[39m. So the repo has two incompatible module worlds and the browser side currently has neither — [38;5;153mlogin[39m is just a
  top-level function. "Other forms will need it later" means I have to pick how they reach the identity, and that choice
  is constrained by something only you know: whether you open this page by double-clicking the file or by serving it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m ES modules are blocked by the browser under [38;5;153mfile://[39m (CORS applies to module scripts). So:

  - [1mES modules ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m)[0m — an [38;5;153mauth.js[39m module other forms [38;5;153mimport[39m. Buys real encapsulation, explicit
    dependencies, no globals, and it's the same shape a bundler would want later. Costs: the page must be served over
    http ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar); double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working.
  - [1mGlobal namespace object[0m — [38;5;153mwindow.Auth = { getUserId, setUserId, clear }[39m from a classic [38;5;153mauth.js[39m script tag. Buys:
    keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working, zero tooling, one-line HTML change. Costs: a global, and load order in the HTML becomes
    load-bearing.
  - [1mBundler[0m — rules itself out. A build step for a two-file stub is YAGNI.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules, if you're willing to serve the page.[0m You've described this growing across multiple
  forms, and explicit imports are what keeps that from turning into load-order bugs. But if double-clicking the file is
  how this thing gets used, say so and the global namespace is the honest answer — it's not a bad design at this size,
  just a less scalable one.
